---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { matrixColumns, matrixRows, matrixReferences } from "../data/erlang-rust-matrix.mjs";
import { matrixCategories, matrixSourceCatalog, matrixRowAnnotations } from "../data/erlang-rust-matrix-navigation.mjs";
import { matrixRowEvidence, evidenceSource, promotionRequirements } from "../data/erlang-rust-evidence.mjs";
import "../styles/erlang-rust-matrix.css";

const erlangExample = "-module(counter).\n-behaviour(gen_server).\n-export([start_link/0, inc/1, init/1, handle_call/3]).\nstart_link() -> gen_server:start_link(?MODULE, 0, []).\ninc(Pid) -> gen_server:call(Pid, inc).\ninit(N) -> {ok, N}.\nhandle_call(inc, _From, N) ->\n    Next = N + 1,\n    {reply, Next, Next}.";
const rustExample = "use tokio::sync::{mpsc, oneshot};\nenum Request { Increment(oneshot::Sender<u64>) }\nasync fn run(mut inbox: mpsc::Receiver<Request>) {\n    let mut count = 0_u64;\n    while let Some(Request::Increment(reply)) = inbox.recv().await {\n        count += 1;\n        let _ = reply.send(count);\n    }\n}";
const oresExample = "# CONCEPTUAL FLOW — NOT ORESLANG SOURCE SYNTAX\nSupervisor starts typed Counter actor with actor-local arena\nCaller transfers an Increment(reply) message into mailbox\nHandler acquires a critical-write loan on actor state\n  next = state.count + 1; state.count = next\nHandler releases state loan before replying\nHandler sends rt copy next to the reply destination\nUnhandled failure -> supervisor restart policy\nNew code -> stage -> validate -> drain -> activate or roll back";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Erlang vs Rust vs Oreslang — ${matrixRows.length}-feature architecture matrix`}
  description="Compare Erlang, Rust and Oreslang across memory safety, compact arena offsets, non-blocking I/O, embedded, FPGA/ASIC, carrier-grade Linux, mobile and desktop alongside actor and systems design."
>
  <section class="page-hero synthesis-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Language comparisons / systems architecture</p>
    <h1>Erlang + Rust, with a different memory and execution model.</h1>
    <p>
      Oreslang takes inspiration from <strong>Erlang's</strong> supervised, message-driven resilience
      and <strong>Rust's</strong> compile-time ownership discipline—then pursues typed, isolated actors,
      data-oriented memory layouts and backend-specific native execution.
    </p>
    <p class="synthesis-promise">
      A design synthesis, <strong>not a claim that Oreslang already matches Erlang/OTP or Rust in production.</strong>
      The public <a href="/language/">feature snapshot</a> does not currently certify support on any backend.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body synthesis-body" aria-label="How Oreslang combines the ideas">
    <div class="synthesis-notes">
      <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">From Erlang</p>
        <h2>Isolation and recovery</h2>
        <p>Actor mailboxes, supervisor trees, recoverable failures and carefully staged code changes—not a promise of OTP protocol or runtime compatibility.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">From Rust</p>
        <h2>Ownership and static safety</h2>
        <p>Typed payloads, explicit borrows and moves, controlled mutation and native memory lifetime management—not a Rust syntax or ABI clone.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">Oreslang's additions</p>
        <h2>Actors meet data locality</h2>
        <p>Actor-owned arenas, critical state loans, CPU-conscious AoS/SoA/AoSoA layouts and target-specific AOT/JIT paths. These remain design and verification work.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="synthesis-how">
      <h2>How the pieces fit together</h2>
      <p>
        A supervisor owns an actor group. Actor mailboxes and configured handler concurrency govern how
        messages are scheduled; multiple asynchronous requests may be in flight, while exclusive state
        loans protect mutation. Typed messages cross actor boundaries;
        mutable actor state is accessed through checked critical loans. The goal is to process bulk numeric
        data in contiguous CPU/GPU-friendly buffers <em>inside</em> well-defined ownership boundaries,
        rather than allocating an actor per data element.
      </p>
      <p>
        An LLVM profile targets explicit actor-local native heaps without tracing GC; a GraalVM profile
        can use off-heap actor regions while still requiring managed GC. Neither target proves real-time
        deadlines or outperforms BEAM/Rust without equal-workload measurement.
      </p>
    </div>


    <section class="synthesis-walkthrough" id="actor-walkthrough" aria-labelledby="actor-walkthrough-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Worked architecture example / a supervised counter</p>
      <h2 id="actor-walkthrough-title">One message, three programming models.</h2>
      <p>
        In each version a client requests an increment and receives a result. The important distinction is not the arithmetic:
        it is where memory safety, message isolation, failure recovery and code replacement are enforced.
      </p>
      <p class="synthesis-walkthrough-warning">
        These are <strong>short explanatory excerpts, not standalone tested programs</strong>.
        Erlang and Rust show representative APIs; the Oreslang column is <strong>conceptual pseudocode, not valid or compiler-verified Oreslang syntax</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="synthesis-example-grid">
        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">Erlang / OTP</p>
          <h3>Process isolation + supervision</h3>
          <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Erlang gen_server example, scrollable code"><code>{erlangExample}</code></pre>
          <p>Run the `gen_server` under an OTP supervisor to apply a restart policy. BEAM controls per-process heaps and tracing GC. A live upgrade requires release/state migration discipline; this excerpt alone does not implement it.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">Rust / Tokio</p>
          <h3>Owned channels + async execution</h3>
          <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Rust Tokio example, scrollable code"><code>{rustExample}</code></pre>
          <p>The compiler checks ownership of the sender and receiver types; the mailbox and executor come from Tokio. Supervision, restart and live upgrades require additional application or framework logic.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">Oreslang / intended model</p>
          <h3>Typed actor + borrow-checked state</h3>
          <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Conceptual Oreslang example, not valid source code"><code>{oresExample}</code></pre>
          <p>The design combines actor-local mutable storage, explicit state loans and supervised failure handling. The compiler must prove message/borrow contracts. Hot reload and cross-backend behavior remain unverified.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="synthesis-flow-summary">
        <h3>What is combined—and what is new</h3>
        <p><strong>From Erlang:</strong> actor failure domains, supervisor policies and code-generation changes.
          <strong>From Rust:</strong> static ownership, checked borrows and low-level native data control.
          <strong>Oreslang's design:</strong> a typed boundary between the two, with actor arenas, explicitly borrowed state,
          and separate CPU-oriented tasks for bulk work instead of one actor per element.</p>
        <p>Multiple asynchronous requests may be in flight under an actor's configured policy; a state write loan must remain exclusive. A completed website build does not prove these runtime semantics.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <header class="synthesis-matrix-intro">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">{matrixRows.length} technical dimensions</p>
        <h2>Side-by-side feature matrix</h2>
        <p>Production facts for established ecosystems are separated from Oreslang design intentions.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="/comparisons/">Read detailed pairwise comparisons →</a>
    </header>
    <form class="synthesis-filter" role="search" aria-label="Find a language comparison" onsubmit="return false">
      <div>
        <label for="synthesis-search">Find a feature</label>
        <input id="synthesis-search" type="search" name="q" autocomplete="off" placeholder="Try supervision, WebSocket, FFI, memory…" aria-controls="synthesis-table-body" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="synthesis-category">Category</label>
        <select id="synthesis-category" name="category" aria-controls="synthesis-table-body">
          {matrixCategories.map((category) => <option value={category.id}>{category.label}</option>)}
        </select>
      </div>
      <button id="synthesis-reset" type="button">Clear filters</button>
    </form>
    <p id="synthesis-results" class="synthesis-results" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">{matrixRows.length} of {matrixRows.length} dimensions shown.</p>
    <div class="synthesis-proof-policy" id="backend-evidence">
      <h2>Backend-specific verification: evidence, not checkboxes</h2>
      <p>Evidence source: <a href={evidenceSource.href}>{evidenceSource.referenceId}</a>
        (updated {evidenceSource.date}, non-normative). A row marked <strong>Not verified</strong>
        has a related entry with no publicly verified compiler conformance.
        <strong>Not assessed</strong> means the public draft does not separately evaluate that topic—not that the feature is absent or impossible.</p>
      <details>
        <summary>What would qualify as partial, tested or production-verified?</summary>
        <ol>{promotionRequirements.map((requirement) => <li>{requirement}</li>)}</ol>
        <p>Approval is per backend and feature. A green website CI run, a historical demo, or an unexecuted PR cannot promote a feature.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

    <p class="synthesis-scroll-hint">On smaller screens, scroll the matrix horizontally to see every language. Each feature has its own shareable link and primary documentation.</p>

    <div class="synthesis-table-scroller" role="region" aria-label="Erlang, Rust, Oreslang and other languages feature comparison" tabindex="0">
      <table class="synthesis-table">
        <caption>Architecture and ecosystem comparison, reviewed October 2026. Oreslang entries describe goals, not verified compiler support.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Feature</th>
            {matrixColumns.map((column) => <th scope="col" class={column.key === "oreslang" ? "synthesis-oreslang-column" : ""}>{column.label}</th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="synthesis-table-body">
          {matrixRows.map((row) => (
            <tr id={row.id} data-category={matrixRowAnnotations[row.id].category}>
              <th scope="row">
                <a class="synthesis-feature-link" href={`#${row.id}`} title={`Link to ${row.feature}`}>{row.feature}<span aria-hidden="true"> ↗</span></a>
                <details class="synthesis-row-sources">
                  <summary>Sources ({matrixRowAnnotations[row.id].sources.length})</summary>
                  <ul>
                    {matrixRowAnnotations[row.id].sources.map((key) => (
                      <li>
                        <a href={matrixSourceCatalog[key].url}
                          rel={matrixSourceCatalog[key].url.startsWith("https://") ? "noopener noreferrer" : undefined}
                          target={matrixSourceCatalog[key].url.startsWith("https://") ? "_blank" : undefined}>
                          {matrixSourceCatalog[key].label}
                        </a>
                      </li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </details>
              </th>
              <td>{Array.isArray(row.erlang) ? <ol>{row.erlang.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ol> : row.erlang}</td>
              <td>{Array.isArray(row.rust) ? <ol>{row.rust.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ol> : row.rust}</td>
              <td class="synthesis-oreslang-column">
                <span class="synthesis-status">{row.status === "research" ? "Research / not verified" : "Design / not verified"}</span>
                <details class="synthesis-verification">
                  <summary>Per-backend evidence</summary>
                  <dl>
                    {matrixRowEvidence[row.id].backends.map((backend: { label: string; labelText: string }) => (
                      <div>
                        <dt>{backend.label}</dt>
                        <dd>{backend.labelText}</dd>
                      </div>
                    ))}
                  </dl>
                  {matrixRowEvidence[row.id].features.length > 0 ? (
                    <p>Covered draft topics: {matrixRowEvidence[row.id].features.map((feature: { name: string }) => feature.name).join("; ")}.</p>
                  ) : (
                    <p>This topic has no separate entry in the public draft; no backend support is inferred.</p>
                  )}
                  <a href={evidenceSource.href}>Inspect source evidence ↗</a>
                </details>
                {Array.isArray(row.oreslang) ? <ol>{row.oreslang.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ol> : row.oreslang}
              </td>
              <td>{Array.isArray(row.others) ? <ol>{row.others.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ol> : row.others}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p id="synthesis-empty" class="synthesis-empty" hidden>No matches. Try another term or clear the filters.</p>

    <section class="synthesis-benchmark" aria-label="How to fairly test system performance">
      <p class="eyebrow">Before comparing performance</p>
      <h2>A fair high-load benchmark needs more than requests per second.</h2>
      <p>
        Erlang, Rust and Oreslang expose different runtime and allocation strategies. Treat the
        following as a <strong>measurement protocol, not published benchmark results</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="synthesis-benchmark-grid">
        <article>
          <h3>Workload parity</h3>
          <p>Match payloads, connection counts, TLS configuration, protocol semantics, restart policy and feature behavior. Record workload code and each exact compiler/runtime revision.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>CPU and memory</h3>
          <p>Report CPU-seconds per completed request, core saturation, RSS peak and steady-state, allocations, actor/process heap usage, collector time and fragmentation.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Tail latency and overload</h3>
          <p>Measure p50/p95/p99/p99.9 response time under open-loop offered load, connection churn, backpressure, queue growth, errors and load shedding. Include warm-up and idle baselines.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Failure and recovery</h3>
          <p>Inject actor crashes, broken FFI calls, abrupt worker death and rolling code upgrades. Record recovery time, dropped or duplicate messages, resource reclamation and service availability.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>Only publish speedups, CPU efficiencies or memory reductions once the workloads, measurement tools, confidence intervals and raw artifacts are reproducible. <a href="/performance/">See existing measurements →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="synthesis-caveats" aria-label="Interpreting the comparison">
      <h2>What these comparisons do not establish</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Soft real time is not hard real time.</strong> Neither Oreslang nor ordinary BEAM or Rust
        deployment makes worst-case latency guarantees by language choice alone. Carrier-grade Linux is a
        platform and operational discipline, not a language certification. GPU and RTC rows distinguish
        orchestration or signaling from accelerator kernels and media processing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>High-load performance needs a reproducible workload.</strong> CPU utilization, RSS/heap
        footprint, allocations, GC pauses, mailbox depth, tail latency and throughput must be measured
        on matching hardware and release configurations before making speed or memory claims.
        See <a href="/performance/">performance evidence and its limitations</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Compact addressing is not a 32-bit address-space guarantee.</strong> A 32-bit arena offset can reduce reference storage on a 64-bit host only when the region bounds, object lifetime, alignment and generation checks are sound. Likewise, an LLVM native binary does not turn software into synthesizable FPGA or ASIC circuitry.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>Verification is backend-specific.</strong> The public Oreslang
        <a href="/language/">draft feature matrix</a> marks Java/GraalVM, LLVM, .NET and WebAssembly
        entries as not verified. Website builds and documentation tests do not substitute for compiler
        conformance, ABI compatibility or operational validation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="synthesis-sources" aria-label="Primary documentation and background">
      <h2>Sources and further reading</h2>
      <p>Official language/runtime documentation where possible; Oreslang claims are explicitly marked as design goals.</p>
      <ul>
        {matrixReferences.map((source) => (
          <li>
            <a href={source.url} rel={source.url.startsWith("https://") ? "noopener noreferrer" : undefined}
              target={source.url.startsWith("https://") ? "_blank" : undefined}>{source.label}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  // Progressive enhancement: all rows and their source links remain visible without JavaScript.
  const search = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("#synthesis-search");
  const category = document.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>("#synthesis-category");
  const reset = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("#synthesis-reset");
  const count = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("#synthesis-results");
  const empty = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("#synthesis-empty");
  const body = document.querySelector<HTMLTableSectionElement>("#synthesis-table-body");
  if (search && category && reset && count && empty && body) {
    const rows = Array.from(body.querySelectorAll<HTMLTableRowElement>("tr[data-category]"));
    const allowed = new Set(Array.from(category.options, (option) => option.value));
    const apply = (writeUrl = false) => {
      const term = search.value.trim().toLocaleLowerCase();
      const selected = allowed.has(category.value) ? category.value : "all";
      let shown = 0;
      for (const row of rows) {
        const matchesCategory = selected === "all" || row.dataset.category === selected;
        const matchesText = (row.textContent ?? "").toLocaleLowerCase().includes(term);
        row.hidden = !(matchesCategory && matchesText);
        if (!row.hidden) shown++;
      }
      count.textContent = `${shown} of ${rows.length} dimensions shown.`;
      empty.hidden = shown > 0;
      if (writeUrl) {
        const url = new URL(window.location.href);
        term ? url.searchParams.set("q", search.value.trim()) : url.searchParams.delete("q");
        selected !== "all" ? url.searchParams.set("category", selected) : url.searchParams.delete("category");
        window.history.replaceState(null, "", url);
      }
    };
    const readUrl = () => {
      const params = new URL(window.location.href).searchParams;
      search.value = params.get("q") ?? "";
      const selected = params.get("category") ?? "all";
      category.value = allowed.has(selected) ? selected : "all";
      apply();
    };
    search.addEventListener("input", () => apply(true));
    category.addEventListener("change", () => apply(true));
    reset.addEventListener("click", () => {
      search.value = "";
      category.value = "all";
      apply(true);
      search.focus();
    });
    window.addEventListener("popstate", readUrl);
    window.addEventListener("hashchange", () => {
      const id = decodeURIComponent(window.location.hash.slice(1));
      const target = rows.find((row) => row.id === id);
      if (target?.hidden) {
        search.value = "";
        category.value = "all";
        apply(true);
      }
    });
    readUrl();
  }
</script>
